<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amir R. Asadi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistical Laboratory, Centre for Mathematical Sciences, University of Cambridge</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cambridge CB3 0WA, United Kingdom</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">asadi@statslab.cam.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Hierarchical structures, which include multiple levels, are prevalent in statistical and machine-learning models as well as physical systems. Extending the foundational result that the maximum entropy distribution under mean constraints is given by the exponential Gibbs-Boltzmann form, we introduce the framework of <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchical maximum entropy</em> to address these multilevel models. We demonstrate that Pareto optimal distributions, which maximize entropies across all levels of hierarchical transformations, can be obtained via renormalization-group procedures from theoretical physics. This is achieved by formulating multilevel extensions of the Gibbs variational principle and the Donsker-Varadhan variational representation of entropy. Moreover, we explore settings with hierarchical invariances that significantly simplify the renormalization-group procedures, enhancing computational efficiency: quadratic modular loss functions, logarithmic loss functions, and nearest-neighbor loss functions. This is accomplished through the introduction of the concept of parameter flows, which serves as an analog to renormalization flows in renormalization group theory. This work connects ideas from probability theory, information theory, and statistical mechanics.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Donsker-Varadhan; Entropy Optimization; Gibbs Variational Principle; Hierarchical Invariance; Renormalization Group.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Maximum Entropy (ME) principle, rooted in information theory and statistical mechanics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, provides a robust framework for modeling probability distributions when only partial information is available. The core idea is to select the distribution that satisfies the known constraints, such as specified averages under a loss function, while making the fewest additional assumptions, by maximizing information entropy. Initially introduced by Claude Shannon and intimately related to thermodynamic entropy, information entropy quantifies the uncertainty inherent in a distribution. By maximizing entropy under the imposed constraints, the ME principle avoids injecting extraneous biases and yields the most uninformative distribution consistent with the data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ME principle and its associated technical tools have been effectively applied across various disciplines. In statistics and data science, it is used in statistical inference from data samples, as discussed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Additionally, ME techniques are utilized in entropic optimal transport and Schrödinger bridges, which are foundational for score-based generative modeling, as explored by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In natural language processing, ME models are employed in tasks such as text classification, part-of-speech tagging, and language modeling. Notable works include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. In probabilistic machine learning, the ME principle underpins entropic regularization techniques, providing principled methods for deriving optimal posterior distributions during model training, as detailed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In physics and statistical mechanics, it is fundamental to the formulation of equilibrium models, a concept introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, the ME principle is closely related to the Principle of Minimum Information Discrimination and the Principle of Maximum Cross Entropy, which are essential in deriving probability distributions that align with given constraints while remaining as unbiased as possible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental result in probability and information theory is the emergence of the Gibbs-Boltzmann distribution as the solution to the entropy maximization problem under moment constraints. Specifically, given a random variable <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a loss function <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (also referred to as the energy function in statistical physics), assume that we seek to determine the probability distribution <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes entropy while satisfying a constraint on the expected value of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be formulated as the optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\argmax_{P_{X}\,:\,\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}H(X)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\argmax_{P_{X}\,:\,\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}H(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
The optimal solution is given by the Gibbs-Boltzmann distribution</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}(x)\propto\exp\left(\lambda L(x)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}(x)\propto\exp\left(\lambda L(x)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where the temperature parameter <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen to satisfy the constraint <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
This result follows from the Gibbs variational principle or the Donsker-Varadhan variational representation of entropy (see Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Gibbs variational principle). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), both of which provide an optimization-based characterization of equilibrium distributions. In this paper, we extend this framework to hierarchical settings, incorporating entropy at multiple levels of a hierarchy and generalizing the classical Gibbs-Boltzmann formulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In many real-world systems, structures exhibit dependencies and interactions across multiple length scales, leading to complex hierarchical organizations. Such multi-scale systems and hierarchical models are ubiquitous in physics, machine learning, and statistics. However, the classical ME framework typically does not account for uncertainty across multiple hierarchies or scales.
Here, we introduce <em id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchical maximum entropy</em>, a framework that generalizes the classical ME framework by integrating hierarchical structures into the entropy maximization problem. This enables us to study distributions that maximize uncertainty over multiple levels of a hierarchy while still satisfying mean constraints. We then show close intrinsic connections with renormalization group theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In physics, problems with multiple scales, such as critical phenomena, are usually analyzed with renormalization group theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
The renormalization group, formulated by Wilson in the 1970s, is a powerful apparatus for studying complex problems involving multiple length scales, particularly critical phenomena in statistical physics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Beyond physics, it has been linked to probability theory, where it helps explain universality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Chapter 10]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The renormalization group provides a systematic approach to analyzing multiscale problems by exploiting the fundamental concept of self-similarity (or scale invariance). Many challenging physics problems arise due to interactions across multiple scales, making direct analysis difficult. The renormalization group tackles this by decomposing a complex, multiscale system into a sequence of simpler steps, each associated with a single characteristic length scale, thus making computations more tractable. Implementation in computers was one of the main motivations of Wilson in developing renormalization group theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. At the heart of this framework is iterative coarse-graining and renormalization of a system, and using the idea of <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">renormalization flow</em>, which describes how model parameters evolve under these iterative transformations. This flow enables systematic exploration of the behavior of the system on different scales, effectively treating scale as an analog of time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Using this structured approach, the renormalization group provides deep insights into the fundamental nature of criticality, universality, and other emergent phenomena in physics and mathematics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we define hierarchical entropy as the linear combination of entropies computed at different levels of a hierarchical system, given a vector of weight coefficients. We study the optimization of hierarchical entropy as an essential step toward our ultimate goal of analyzing the framework of hierarchical maximum entropy.
There are conceptual similarities between the definition of hierarchical entropy and what the works by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> refer to as “phase space complexity” in statistical mechanics. The characterization of maximum phase space complexity distributions in general settings was left as an open question in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, where it was suggested that a renormalization group approach could be considered to evaluate complexity as a function of temperature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Contributions of the Paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate the properties and applications of hierarchical maximum entropy. Our contributions are specifically as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formally define the hierarchical maximum entropy problem and develop a rigorous theoretical framework for its analysis. We also study the related problem of hierarchical minimum relative entropy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that solutions to this problem can be constructed via the renormalization group procedure in conjunction with disintegration operations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify several settings where hierarchical invariances simplify the iterative renormalization group procedure by introducing the concept of parameter flows, leading to significant computational advantages in computing the optimal distributions. In particular, we analyze cases involving quadratic modular loss functions, logarithmic loss functions, and nearest-neighbor loss functions.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By bridging ideas from probability theory, information theory, and statistical mechanics, our work offers new insights into the entropy-maximizing distributions in hierarchical systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Organization of the Paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> offers several notations, preliminary definitions, and tools. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we introduce the hierarchical maximum entropy problem, providing formal definitions and a detailed problem setup. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the iterative renormalization group procedure used to solve the hierarchical maximum entropy problem along with related results. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Hierarchical Invariances and Parameter Flows ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we present examples where hierarchical invariances arise: (i) quadratic modular loss functions, using modular multivariate Gaussians, (ii) logarithmic loss functions, showcasing Dirichlet distributions, and (iii) nearest-neighbor loss functions, utilizing the self-similarity of the one-dimensional Ising model. For each example, we analyze the parameter flows and the behavior of the iterative procedures. Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> discusses the related hierarchical relative entropy minimization problem. Finally, Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Conclusion ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> concludes the paper, summarizing the main findings and discussing potential future research directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given any measurable mapping <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and any measure <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the pushforward measure of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\#}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\#}\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P</annotation></semantics></math> to denote that random variable <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has distribution <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The notation <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}\ll Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}\ll Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> signifies that the distribution <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous with respect to the distribution <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive definite matrix. The notation <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Dir</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a Dirichlet distribution with parameters <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability distribution defined on <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a random variable (or vector) with distribution <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete probability distribution, the (Shannon) entropy of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(X)=H(P_{X})=-\sum_{x\in\mathcal{A}}P_{X}(x)\log P_{X}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)=H(P_{X})=-\sum_{x\in\mathcal{A}}P_{X}(x)\log P_{X}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}(x)\log P_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}(x)\log P_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is understood to be <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous probability distribution with probability density function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the (differential) entropy of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(X)=H(P_{X})=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)\,\mathrm{d}x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)=H(P_{X})=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is understood to be <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> wherever <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, all logarithms are in the natural base. Therefore, all entropies are in nat units.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The relative entropy (or Kullback-Leibler divergence) between two probability distributions <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the same set <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\mathrm{d}P_{X}}{\mathrm{d}Q_{X}}(X)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\mathrm{d}P_{X}}{\mathrm{d}Q_{X}}(X)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}P_{X}}{\mathrm{d}Q_{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}P_{X}}{\mathrm{d}Q_{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Radon-Nikodym derivative if <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}\ll Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}\ll Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conditional relative entropy between <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y|X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y|X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{Y|X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{Y|X}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{Y|X}\|Q_{Y|X}|P_{X}):=\mathbb{E}\left[D(P_{Y|X}(\cdot|X)\|Q_{Y|X}(\cdot|X))\right],\qquad X\sim P_{X}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{Y|X}\|Q_{Y|X}|P_{X}):=\mathbb{E}\left[D(P_{Y|X}(\cdot|X)\|Q_{Y|X}(\cdot|X))\right],\qquad X\sim P_{X}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following result, relating the relative entropy and conditional relative entropy, is known as the chain rule.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chain rule)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be two random vectors. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X,Y)=H(X)+H(Y|X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X,Y)=H(X)+H(Y|X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{XY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{XY}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{XY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{XY}</annotation></semantics></math> are two joint distributions of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{XY}\|Q_{XY})=D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+D(P_{Y|X}\|Q_{Y|X}|P_{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{XY}\|Q_{XY})=D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+D(P_{Y|X}\|Q_{Y|X}|P_{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now state the following well-known result, closely related to the Donsker-Varadhan variational representation of entropy, which we will later extend to hierarchical settings:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Gibbs variational principle)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}:=\int_{x\in{\mathcal{A}}}\exp(-\lambda f(x))\,\mathrm{d}x&lt;\infty," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}:=\int_{x\in{\mathcal{A}}}\exp(-\lambda f(x))\,\mathrm{d}x&lt;\infty,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous set. Then for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]=\log\tilde{Z}-D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]=\log\tilde{Z}-D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution with probability density function</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp(-\lambda f(x))}{Z},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>Z</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp(-\lambda f(x))}{Z},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An analogous counterpart to Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Gibbs variational principle). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> applies to discrete random variables by replacing integrals with sums and interpreting <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as Shannon entropy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-objective (vector) optimization problems, we consider several conflicting objectives simultaneously. Unlike single-objective optimization, where the goal is to find the best solution, multi-objective optimization seeks solutions that balance trade-offs among different criteria. In such settings, a solution is considered desirable if it is not possible to improve one objective without causing a decline in another. This idea leads to the concept of Pareto optimality, which provides a framework for evaluating and comparing solutions based on whether one solution ‘dominates’ another. With this understanding, we can now present the precise definition of Pareto optimality and the Pareto front.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Pareto Optimality and Pareto Front)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a feasible set and let <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{i}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}\}_{i=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}\}_{i=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of objective functions. A point <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ast}\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ast}\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pareto optimal</em> and denoted with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ast}=\argmax_{x\in\Omega}~(f_{1}(x),\dots,f_{d}(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ast}=\argmax_{x\in\Omega}~(f_{1}(x),\dots,f_{d}(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">if there is no other point <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(x)\geq f_{i}(x^{\ast})\quad\text{for all }i=1,\dots,d," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(x)\geq f_{i}(x^{\ast})\quad\text{for all }i=1,\dots,d,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with the strict inequality</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}(x)&gt;f_{j}(x^{\ast})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}(x)&gt;f_{j}(x^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">holding for at least one index <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\dots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\dots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, no other feasible point can improve one objective without causing a deterioration in at least one other objective. The <em id="Thmdefinition4.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pareto front</em> is the set of all Pareto optimal points, that is,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{x\in\Omega:x\text{ is Pareto optimal}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is Pareto optimal</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{x\in\Omega:x\text{ is Pareto optimal}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation emphasizes that a Pareto optimal point cannot be dominated by any other feasible point across all objectives, aligning with the general concept of efficiency in multi-objective optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hierarchical Maximum Entropy</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable or a random vector taking values from the set <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents data or the state of a system. Consider a hierarchical sequence of (coarse-graining) transformations <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}:=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}:=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we recursively define</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i+1)}:=T_{i}\bigl(X^{(i)}\bigr),\quad\textrm{for all }1\leq i\leq d-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i+1)}:=T_{i}\bigl(X^{(i)}\bigr),\quad\textrm{for all }1\leq i\leq d-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(1)}:=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(1)}:=X</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the distribution of <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> may represent the pixel values of an image, where each transformation <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> computes the average of neighboring pixel groups and outputs a single pixel per group, producing a lower-resolution image. Consequently, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(2)},\dots,X^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(2)},\dots,X^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> are progressively lower-resolution representations of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Another example of a transformation sequence <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decimation</em>, where a subset of random variables is removed at each stage, leaving the rest intact. For example, let <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> represent a collection of <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> blocks. In this context, <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> could correspond to the synaptic weights of an artificial neural network, with each block representing an individual layer. The transformations <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> progressively reduce <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by simply eliminating the blocks sequentially, starting from <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, the reduced representation is given by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i)}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{d-i+1}),\quad\textrm{for }1\leq i\leq d." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i)}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{d-i+1}),\quad\textrm{for }1\leq i\leq d.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the context of renormalization group theory, decimation is a scaling transformation introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Beyond physics, decimation also appears in cartography, where it is employed to simplify geographic maps of a region. As one zooms out, smaller cities are omitted, producing maps at progressively coarser scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the sequel, assuming that all probability spaces are standard, we address the following multi-objective (vector) optimization problem, which we call hierarchical maximum entropy:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\argmax_{P_{X}:~\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}\bigl(H\bigl(P_{X^{(1)}}\bigr),H\bigl(P_{X^{(2)}}\bigr),\dots,H\bigl(P_{X^{(d)}}\bigr)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.548em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmax_{P_{X}:~\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}\bigl(H\bigl(P_{X^{(1)}}\bigr),H\bigl(P_{X^{(2)}}\bigr),\dots,H\bigl(P_{X^{(d)}}\bigr)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\bigl(P_{X^{(i)}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\bigl(P_{X^{(i)}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the entropy of the <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th transformed random vector. The objective is to identify Pareto optimal distributions <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\ast}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\ast}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> that simultaneously maximize the entropies across all levels of the hierarchy.
Clearly, the classical maximum entropy problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1.1 Background ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is a special case of (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 3.1 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) when <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any given <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}=(\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}=(\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as positive real value for all <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <em id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchical entropy</em> as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{(\mathbf{\sigma},\mathbf{T})}(X):=\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\sigma_{i}}H\left(X^{(i)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{(\mathbf{\sigma},\mathbf{T})}(X):=\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\sigma_{i}}H\left(X^{(i)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We call <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> the hierarchical coefficients vector, where each <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the coefficient at level <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="Thmdefinition5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, let the accumulated hierarchical coefficients be defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\sigma}_{i}:=\sum_{k=1}^{i}\sigma_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\sigma}_{i}:=\sum_{k=1}^{i}\sigma_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To solve (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 3.1 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), we employ the linear scalarization technique from multi-objective optimization literature (for a formal definition of linear scalarization, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>).
Specifically, we aim to solve:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\argmax_{P_{X}:\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}H_{(\mathbf{\sigma},\mathbf{T})}(X)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\argmax_{P_{X}:\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}H_{(\mathbf{\sigma},\mathbf{T})}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are arbitrary scalar weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The distribution <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Pareto optimal solution to (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 3.1 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) if and only if there exist positive weights <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3.3 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the entropy <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> is concave in <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear in <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> is concave in <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, since the objectives are all concave in <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the set of all distributions <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, linear scalarization constructs the entire Pareto front <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Section 3.1]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the constrained optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3.3 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), the Lagrangian is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(P_{X})=H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(P_{X})=H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrange multiplier.
Next, we study the solution to the following associated problem, which we call the hierarchical entropy regularization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmax_{P_{X}}\left\{H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmax_{P_{X}}\left\{H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Renormalization Group</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following definition of a probability operator.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Renormalization)<span id="Thmdefinition6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a continuous distribution on a set <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability density function <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Given inputs <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the renormalization operator <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{X},Z)=\mathcal{R}(P_{X};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{X},Z)=\mathcal{R}(P_{X};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=\int_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{X}(x))^{\theta}\mathrm{d}x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=\int_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{X}(x))^{\theta}\mathrm{d}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution with probability density function</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{X}(x):=\frac{(p_{X}(x))^{\theta}}{Z},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mi>Z</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{X}(x):=\frac{(p_{X}(x))^{\theta}}{Z},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The renormalization operator is defined analogously for discrete distributions except by replacing the integral with a sum.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The distribution <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above is also known as the escort distribution in the statistical physics literature; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the definition of accumulated hierarchical coefficients in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 3.2 ‣ Definition 5. ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) and
consider the iterative sequence of pushforwards (coarse-grainings) and renormalizations in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Assume that all alphabets are standard Borel spaces, which guarantees the existence of regular conditional probabilities and reverse random transformations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
By disintegrating distributions <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}:=P^{(d)}_{X^{(d)}}P^{(d-1)}_{X^{(d-1)}|X^{(d)}}\cdots P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}:=P^{(d)}_{X^{(d)}}P^{(d-1)}_{X^{(d-1)}|X^{(d)}}\cdots P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, let</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(\lambda):=\prod_{i=1}^{d}Z_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(\lambda):=\prod_{i=1}^{d}Z_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The first main result of this section, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> below, shows that <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 3.4 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\bigl(\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\bigr)=\log Z(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\bigl(\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\bigr)=\log Z(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Renormalization Group</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">let</span> <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}:=\int_{\mathcal{X}}\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}:=\int_{\mathcal{X}}\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}(x):=\frac{\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}}{Z_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}(x):=\frac{\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}}{Z_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Initialization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1\text{ to }d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1\text{ to }d-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}\leftarrow(T_{i})_{\#}P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}\leftarrow(T_{i})_{\#}P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Pushforward (Coarse-graining)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(P^{(i+1)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Z_{i+1}\right)\leftarrow\mathcal{R}\left(U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}};\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(P^{(i+1)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Z_{i+1}\right)\leftarrow\mathcal{R}\left(U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}};\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Renormalization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving this theorem, we need the following lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be two continuous distributions taking values on the set <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{Q}_{X},\tilde{Z}):=\mathcal{R}\left(Q_{X};\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{Q}_{X},\tilde{Z}):=\mathcal{R}\left(Q_{X};\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of the renormalization operator. We have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(X)-\theta D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\log\tilde{Z}-(1+\theta)D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{Q}_{X}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)-\theta D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})=\log\tilde{Z}-(1+\theta)D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{Q}_{X}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability density functions of <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We can write</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H(X)-\theta D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(X)-\theta D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x-\theta\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log p_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x-\theta\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{\left(p_{X}(x)\right)^{1+\theta}}{(q_{X}(x))^{\theta}}\right)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{\left(p_{X}(x)\right)^{1+\theta}}{(q_{X}(x))^{\theta}}\right)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-(1+\theta)\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{(q_{X}(x))^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}}\right)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-(1+\theta)\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{(q_{X}(x))^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}}\right)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\tilde{Z}-(1+\theta)D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{Q}_{X}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\tilde{Z}-(1+\theta)D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{Q}_{X}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">An analogous result applies to discrete random variables, where <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(P)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to Shannon entropy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The following result can be viewed as an extension of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1 (Chain rule). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, representing the chain rule of relative entropy with respect to an arbitrary mapping.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X_{2}}=T_{\#}P_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X_{2}}=T_{\#}P_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X_{2}}=T_{\#}Q_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X_{2}}=T_{\#}Q_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{1}})=D(P_{X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{2}})+D(P_{X_{1}|X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}|X_{2}}|P_{X_{2}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{1}})=D(P_{X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{2}})+D(P_{X_{1}|X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}|X_{2}}|P_{X_{2}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X_{1}X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}X_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X_{1}X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}X_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> in two different ways based on the chain rule of relative entropy gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D(P_{X_{1}X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}X_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D(P_{X_{1}X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}X_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=D(P_{X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{2}})+D(P_{X_{1}|X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}|X_{2}}|P_{X_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=D(P_{X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{2}})+D(P_{X_{1}|X_{2}}\|Q_{X_{1}|X_{2}}|P_{X_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=D(P_{X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{1}})+D(P_{X_{2}|X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{2}|X_{1}}|P_{X_{1}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=D(P_{X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{1}})+D(P_{X_{2}|X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{2}|X_{1}}|P_{X_{1}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">The conclusion follows from noting that <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X_{2}|X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{2}|X_{1}}|P_{X_{1}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X_{2}|X_{1}}\|Q_{X_{2}|X_{1}}|P_{X_{1}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hierarchical relative entropy between any two distributions <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X}):=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\sigma_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(i)}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X}):=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\sigma_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(i)}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the pushforward measures when <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through the sequence of transformations <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The result of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> yields the following corollary that provides alternative representations of hierarchical entropies using conditional entropies and accumulated hierarchical coefficients:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X):=\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}H\left(X^{(i)}\middle|X^{(i+1)}\right)+\bar{\sigma}_{d}H\left(X^{(d)}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X):=\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}H\left(X^{(i)}\middle|X^{(i+1)}\right)+\bar{\sigma}_{d}H\left(X^{(d)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X}):=\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i)}|X^{(i+1)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(i)}|X^{(i+1)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i+1)}}\right)+\bar{\sigma}_{d}D\left(P_{X^{(d)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(d)}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X}):=\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i)}|X^{(i+1)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(i)}|X^{(i+1)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i+1)}}\right)+\bar{\sigma}_{d}D\left(P_{X^{(d)}}\middle\|Q_{{X}^{(d)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we are ready to give the first main result of this section, which can be interpreted as a hierarchical extension of the Gibbs variational principle and the Donsker-Varadhan variational representations of entropy.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and equations (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 4.2 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). For any <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\log Z(\lambda)-D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\Bigl(P_{X}\Big\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\log Z(\lambda)-D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\Bigl(P_{X}\Big\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Based on induction on <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we first prove that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}H\bigl(X^{(i)}\bigr)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}H\bigl(X^{(i)}\bigr)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k)}}\middle\|P^{(k)}_{X^{(k)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k)}}\middle\|P^{(k)}_{X^{(k)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, based on Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Gibbs variational principle). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{1}H(X^{(1)})-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{1}H(X^{(1)})-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log Z_{1}-\sigma_{1}D\left(P_{X^{(1)}}\middle\|P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log Z_{1}-\sigma_{1}D\left(P_{X^{(1)}}\middle\|P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log Z_{1}-\bar{\sigma}_{1}D\left(P_{X^{(1)}}\middle\|P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log Z_{1}-\bar{\sigma}_{1}D\left(P_{X^{(1)}}\middle\|P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that (<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 4.5 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) holds for an arbitrary <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We deduce that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\sigma_{i}H\left(X^{(i)}\right)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\sigma_{i}H\left(X^{(i)}\right)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma_{k+1}H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma_{k+1}H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k)}}\middle\|P^{(k)}_{X^{(k)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k)}}\middle\|P^{(k)}_{X^{(k)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma_{k+1}H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma_{k+1}H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|U^{(k)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|U^{(k)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}+\sigma_{k+1}\left(H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{k}}{\sigma_{k+1}}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|U^{(k)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\log Z_{i}+\sigma_{k+1}\left(H\left(X^{(k+1)}\right)-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{k}}{\sigma_{k+1}}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|U^{(k)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad-\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad-\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\log Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\log Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k+1}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|P^{(k+1)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad-\left(\bar{\sigma}_{k+1}D\left(P_{X^{(k+1)}}\middle\|P^{(k+1)}_{X^{(k+1)}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i-1)}_{X^{(i-1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">where (<a href="#S4.E6" title="Equation 4.6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>) follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and (<a href="#S4.E7" title="Equation 4.7 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>) follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
which proves (<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 4.5 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) for <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and completes the inductive argument.
Therefore, for <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=d</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\log Z_{i}-\bar{\sigma}_{d}D\left(P_{X^{(d)}}\middle\|P^{(d)}_{X^{(d)}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i+1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i)}_{{X}^{(i+1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\log Z_{i}-\bar{\sigma}_{d}D\left(P_{X^{(d)}}\middle\|P^{(d)}_{X^{(d)}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}\bar{\sigma}_{i}D\left(P_{X^{(i+1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle\|P^{(i)}_{{X}^{(i+1)}|X^{(i)}}\middle|P_{X^{(i)}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log Z-D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log Z-D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.5.1" class="ltx_text">where (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 4.8 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>) follows from Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the non-negativity of hierarchical relative entropy, we can immediately deduce the following result:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The distribution <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique solution to the maximization problem (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 3.4 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{P_{X}}\left\{H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}=\log Z(\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{P_{X}}\left\{H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)-\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}=\log Z(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Lagrange multiplier for which <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}</annotation></semantics></math> solves the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3.3 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). The following result gives a condition that characterizes <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing it to be solved for explicitly:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L(X)]</annotation></semantics></math> are differentiable with respect to <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the condition</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=-\mu." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=-\mu.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 8. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the optimality condition reads</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\Bigl(H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}\bigr)-\lambda^{\ast}\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}}[L(X)]\Bigr)\Bigg|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\Bigl(H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}\bigr)-\lambda^{\ast}\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}}[L(X)]\Bigr)\Bigg|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding the derivative gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}\bigr)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}-\lambda^{\ast}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}}[L(X)]\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}\bigr)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}-\lambda^{\ast}\,\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda]}}[L(X)]\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.3.1" class="ltx_text">Noting that the derivative of <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log Z(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log Z(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=\frac{\rm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\bigr)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}-\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}_{X}}[L(X)]-\lambda^{\ast}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}}[L(X)]\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=\frac{\rm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}H_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\bigl(\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\bigr)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}-\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}_{X}}[L(X)]-\lambda^{\ast}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}}[L(X)]\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.4.1" class="ltx_text">we immediately obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=-\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}}[L(X)]=-\mu," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=-\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}}[L(X)]=-\mu,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.5.1" class="ltx_text">which completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> can be interpreted as follows:
When we differentiate the maximum value <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log Z(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log Z(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the Lagrange multiplier <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we do not need to account for how the optimal distribution <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> itself changes as <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> varies. This is connected with the envelope theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which states that since the chosen distribution already maximizes the objective at each fixed <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, any infinitesimal change in <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> only affects the optimal value through its direct appearance in the objective function. In other words, there is no first-order contribution from the variation of the optimizer itself.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Hierarchical Invariances and Parameter Flows</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present three scenarios where hierarchical invariances emerge, resulting in a substantial simplification of the iterative procedures from the previous section and notable computational benefits. Drawing inspiration from the concept of renormalization flow in renormalization group theory, we introduce parameter flow formulations for each case.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Quadratic Modular Loss Function</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For some positive integers <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=kd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=kd</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> take values on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the loss function is a definite quadratic function <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(X)=X^{\top}QX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(X)=X^{\top}QX</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive definite matrix. The vector <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is partitioned into <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, each of size <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}=({X}_{[1]},{X}_{[2]},\ldots,{X}_{[d]})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}=({X}_{[1]},{X}_{[2]},\ldots,{X}_{[d]}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each block is defined <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X}_{[i]}:=(X_{(i-1)k+1},X_{(i-1)k+2},\ldots,X_{ik})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X}_{[i]}:=(X_{(i-1)k+1},X_{(i-1)k+2},\ldots,X_{ik})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,d</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, we define decimation <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{(d-i+1)k}\to\mathbb{R}^{(d-i)k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{(d-i+1)k}\to\mathbb{R}^{(d-i)k}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\left({X}_{[1]},\ldots,{X}_{[d-i]},{X}_{[d-i+1]}\right)=\left({X}_{[1]},\ldots,{X}_{[d-i]}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\left({X}_{[1]},\ldots,{X}_{[d-i]},{X}_{[d-i+1]}\right)=\left({X}_{[1]},\ldots,{X}_{[d-i]}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Given the specified loss function, the initial distribution <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is a multivariate Gaussian. Due to the closure properties of multivariate Gaussians under marginalization and renormalization, it follows that each <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains a multivariate Gaussian. Moreover, since multivariate Gaussians are also closed under conditioning and disintegration, the final distribution <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\ast}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\ast}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) is guaranteed to be multivariate Gaussian as well. Consequently, in this case, Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reduces to computing the mean vector and covariance matrix of the distributions <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>, marginalization requires computing the covariance matrix and inverting the large precision matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be computationally prohibitive. In the following, we present an example of hierarchical invariance and self-similarity that allows us to bypass this computational burden.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the symmetric type of multivariate Gaussian distributions defined as follows. This type of distribution possesses <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modularity</em>, a universal property of complex systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given square matrices <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with the same size and any integer <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the following block Toeplitz matrix</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]:=\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B^{T}&amp;B^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
B&amp;A&amp;B^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
B&amp;B&amp;A&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
B&amp;B&amp;B&amp;\cdots&amp;A\end{pmatrix}_{i\times i\textrm{ blocks}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> blocks</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]:=\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B^{T}&amp;B^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
B&amp;A&amp;B^{T}&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
B&amp;B&amp;A&amp;\cdots&amp;B^{T}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
B&amp;B&amp;B&amp;\cdots&amp;A\end{pmatrix}_{i\times i\textrm{ blocks}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We call a multivariate Gaussian distribution that has a precision matrix of the form above a <em id="Thmdefinition8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modular</em> multivariate Gaussian distribution.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the Schur complement theorem implies that if <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before giving the main result of this subsection, we need the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> be a modular multivariate Gaussian distribution with the precision matrix <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric invertible matrix. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})</annotation></semantics></math> is also a modular multivariate Gaussian distribution with the precision matrix <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A^{\prime},B^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A^{\prime},B^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}A^{\prime}=A-B^{T}A^{-1}B,\\
B^{\prime}=B-B^{T}A^{-1}B.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}A^{\prime}=A-B^{T}A^{-1}B,\\
B^{\prime}=B-B^{T}A^{-1}B.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\mathcal{T}_{i}[A,B]</annotation></semantics></math>. The covariance matrix of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math>
is</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}N_{11}&amp;N_{12}\\
N_{21}&amp;N_{22}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}N_{11}&amp;N_{12}\\
N_{21}&amp;N_{22}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where we have denoted the upper left <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i-1)\times(i-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i-1)\times(i-1)</annotation></semantics></math> blocks with <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{11}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same size as <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the well-known marginalization property of multivariate Gaussian distributions, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})</annotation></semantics></math> is also a multivariate Gaussian distribution with covariance matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{11}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If we assume that
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\begin{pmatrix}M_{11}&amp;M_{12}\\
M_{21}&amp;M_{22}\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\begin{pmatrix}M_{11}&amp;M_{12}\\
M_{21}&amp;M_{22}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, where <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\times p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\times p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times q</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, with <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=p+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=p+q</annotation></semantics></math>, then from a classic result in linear algebra we have
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{11}^{-1}=M_{11}-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}M_{21}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>11</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>22</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{11}^{-1}=M_{11}-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}M_{21}.</annotation></semantics></math> Therefore, the precision matrix of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{[1]},\dots,X_{[i-1]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{[1]},\dots,X_{[i-1]})</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N_{11}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>11</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N_{11}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=M_{11}-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}M_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>22</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=M_{11}-M_{12}M_{22}^{-1}M_{21}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A,B]-\begin{pmatrix}B^{T}\\
\vdots\\
B^{T}\end{pmatrix}A^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}B&amp;\cdots&amp;B\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A,B]-\begin{pmatrix}B^{T}\\
\vdots\\
B^{T}\end{pmatrix}A^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}B&amp;\cdots&amp;B\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A^{\prime},B^{\prime}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{T}_{i-1}[A^{\prime},B^{\prime}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that since <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{T}A^{-1}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{T}A^{-1}B</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric, <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> remains symmetric as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is not symmetric, the covariance matrix of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({X}_{[1]},\dots,{X}_{[i-1]})</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessarily retain the modular structure described in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition8" title="Definition 8. ‣ 5.1 Quadratic Modular Loss Function ‣ 5 Hierarchical Invariances and Parameter Flows ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, in contrast to the precision matrices established above.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Parameter flow)<span id="Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the loss function be <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(X)=X^{\top}QX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(X)=X^{\top}QX</annotation></semantics></math>, where the matrix <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\mathcal{T}_{d}[A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\mathcal{T}_{d}[A,B]</annotation></semantics></math> is modular, symmetric, and positive definite. For <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,\dots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,\dots,d</annotation></semantics></math>, let the matrices <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> evolve according to the recursive relations:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}A_{i}=A_{i-1}-B_{i-1}^{T}A_{i-1}^{-1}B_{i-1},\\
B_{i}=B_{i-1}-B_{i-1}^{T}A_{i-1}^{-1}B_{i-1},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}A_{i}=A_{i-1}-B_{i-1}^{T}A_{i-1}^{-1}B_{i-1},\\
B_{i}=B_{i-1}-B_{i-1}^{T}A_{i-1}^{-1}B_{i-1},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the initial conditions are given by</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}A_{1}=A,\\
B_{1}=B.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}A_{1}=A,\\
B_{1}=B.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, all distributions <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> have probability density functions of the similar form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\propto\exp\left(-X^{T}Q_{i}X\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\propto\exp\left(-X^{T}Q_{i}X\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}=\mathcal{T}_{d-i+1}\bigl[\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}A_{i},\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}B_{i}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}=\mathcal{T}_{d-i+1}\bigl[\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}A_{i},\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}B_{i}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math> is modular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem shows that to determine the distributions <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to compute the sub-blocks <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},B_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},B_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> recursively, starting from <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{1},B_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{1},B_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, for large <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the size of these matrices, <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, is much smaller than that of the full precision matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, which is of size <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kd</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, exploiting this hierarchical invariance significantly reduces computational complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Lagrange multiplier for which <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}</annotation></semantics></math> solves the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3.3 ‣ 3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). We have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda^{\ast}=\frac{1}{\mu}\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}\frac{k(d-i)\sigma_{i+1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>μ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda^{\ast}=\frac{1}{\mu}\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}\frac{k(d-i)\sigma_{i+1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{kd}}\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}x^{\top}Qx\right)}\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{kd}}\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}x^{\top}Qx\right)}\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\frac{\sigma_{1}}{\lambda}\right)^{kd/2}\frac{\pi^{kd/2}}{\sqrt{\det Q}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\frac{\sigma_{1}}{\lambda}\right)^{kd/2}\frac{\pi^{kd/2}}{\sqrt{\det Q}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{1}=-\frac{kd}{2\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{1}=-\frac{kd}{2\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the precision matrix of <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}^{\prime}:=\mathcal{T}_{d-i}\Bigl[\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}A_{i+1},\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}B_{i+1}\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}^{\prime}:=\mathcal{T}_{d-i}\Bigl[\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}A_{i+1},\frac{\lambda}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}B_{i+1}\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(x)=\frac{\sqrt{\det Q^{\prime}_{i}}}{(2\pi)^{k(d-i)/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}x^{\top}Q^{\prime}_{i}x\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(x)=\frac{\sqrt{\det Q^{\prime}_{i}}}{(2\pi)^{k(d-i)/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}x^{\top}Q^{\prime}_{i}x\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">denote the probability density of <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can compute</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k(d-i)}}f_{i}(x)^{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}}\,\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k(d-i)}}f_{i}(x)^{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}}\,\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\frac{\sqrt{\det Q^{\prime}_{i}}}{(2\pi)^{k(d-i)/2}}\right)^{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k(d-i)}}\exp\left(-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}x^{\top}Q^{\prime}_{i}x\right)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\frac{\sqrt{\det Q^{\prime}_{i}}}{(2\pi)^{k(d-i)/2}}\right)^{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k(d-i)}}\exp\left(-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}x^{\top}Q^{\prime}_{i}x\right)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(2\pi)^{\frac{k(d-i)}{2}\bigl(1-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\bigr)}\Bigl(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\Bigr)^{-\frac{k(d-i)}{2}}(\det Q^{\prime}_{i})^{\frac{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}-1}{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(2\pi)^{\frac{k(d-i)}{2}\bigl(1-\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\bigr)}\Bigl(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\Bigr)^{-\frac{k(d-i)}{2}}(\det Q^{\prime}_{i})^{\frac{\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}-1}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, based on (<a href="#S5.E3" title="Equation 5.3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Quadratic Modular Loss Function ‣ 5 Hierarchical Invariances and Parameter Flows ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>), we derive</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z_{i+1}=-\frac{k(d-i)\sigma_{i+1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}\lambda}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z_{i+1}=-\frac{k(d-i)\sigma_{i+1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">Invoking Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we complete the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the loss function includes a linear term, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(X)=X^{T}QX+g^{T}X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(X)=X^{T}QX+g^{T}X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then, we can rewrite the loss function as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(X)=(X-\mu)^{T}Q(X-\mu)+C," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(X)=(X-\mu)^{T}Q(X-\mu)+C,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant independent of <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and mean <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> can be efficiently computed using the Block-Levinson recursion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> by solving</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2Q\mu=g." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2Q\mu=g.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">With this reformulation, we can directly apply the theorem stated above to shifted versions of the distributions by replacing <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hessian of residual neural networks, evaluated at the origin with respect to the parameters, exhibits the block Toeplitz structure <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{d}[A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{d}[A,B]</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Consequently, when the empirical loss function of the neural network is approximated by its second-order Taylor expansion–equivalently, when the generalized posterior is approximated as a multivariate Gaussian–it possesses the invariance property established in the previous theorem.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Logarithmic Loss Function</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a positive integer <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the space be</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\{{x}=(x_{1},\dots,x_{N}):x_{1}+\dots+x_{N}=1,0\leq x_{i}\leq 1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\{{x}=(x_{1},\dots,x_{N}):x_{1}+\dots+x_{N}=1,0\leq x_{i}\leq 1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the hierarchical transformation <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{i}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right):=\left(x_{1}+x_{2},x_{3}+x_{4},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}-1}+x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{i}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right):=\left(x_{1}+x_{2},x_{3}+x_{4},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}-1}+x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log x^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log x^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the component-wise logarithm of vector <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result shows hierarchical invariance in this setting:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Parameter flow)<span id="Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the loss function has the logarithmic form:
<math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L({x})=-\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\log x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝛂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L({x})=-\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\log x</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector with all non-negative entries. Then, all distributions <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> have probability density functions of the similar form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\propto\exp\left(-\boldsymbol{\alpha_{(i)}}^{\top}\log x^{(i)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒊</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\propto\exp\left(-\boldsymbol{\alpha_{(i)}}^{\top}\log x^{(i)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,\dots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,\dots,d</annotation></semantics></math>, the vectors <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛂</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> evolve according to the recursive relations:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}=\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i-1}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}T_{i-1}\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i-1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(i-1)}\right)-\boldsymbol{1}_{(i)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}=\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i-1}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}T_{i-1}\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i-1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(i-1)}\right)-\boldsymbol{1}_{(i)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{1}_{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{1}_{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the all-one vector with the same length as <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛂</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the initial condition is given by</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}=\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}\boldsymbol{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}=\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}\boldsymbol{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by noting that</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{X^{(1)}}^{(1)}({x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{X^{(1)}}^{(1)}({x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L({x})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L({x})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\exp\left(\frac{\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{N}\alpha_{i}\log x_{i}}{\sigma_{1}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\exp\left(\frac{\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{N}\alpha_{i}\log x_{i}}{\sigma_{1}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prod_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{\frac{\lambda\alpha_{i}}{\sigma_{1}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prod_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{\frac{\lambda\alpha_{i}}{\sigma_{1}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X^{(1)}}^{(1)}({x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X^{(1)}}^{(1)}({x})</annotation></semantics></math> follows a Dirichlet distribution with concentration parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}.</annotation></semantics></math>
To complete the result, we apply the aggregation property of Dirichlet distributions, which states that if <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector with all entries larger than or equal to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
if</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})\sim\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Dir</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})\sim\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{N}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">then, replacing two adjacent components <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by their sum
results in another Dirichlet distribution as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{i}+X_{i+1},\dots,X_{N})\sim\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{i+1},\dots,\alpha_{N})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Dir</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{i}+X_{i+1},\dots,X_{N})\sim\text{Dir}(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{i+1},\dots,\alpha_{N}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Using this property,
we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\sim\text{Dir}\bigl(T_{1}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}\bigr)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Dir</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\sim\text{Dir}\bigl(T_{1}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}\bigr)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Based on this result, we deduce that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(2)}_{X^{(2)}}\sim\text{Dir}\Bigl(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{1}}{\bar{\sigma}_{2}}T_{1}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Dir</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(2)}_{X^{(2)}}\sim\text{Dir}\Bigl(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{1}}{\bar{\sigma}_{2}}T_{1}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{(1)}+\boldsymbol{1}_{(1)}\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is completed by applying this argument inductively.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Nearest-Neighbors Loss Function</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a positive integer <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the space be
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\{-1,+1\}^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\{-1,+1\}^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math> For all <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the hierarchical transformation <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\frac{N}{2^{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{i}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right):=\left(x_{1},x_{3},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}-1}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{i}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}}\right):=\left(x_{1},x_{3},\dots,x_{\frac{N}{2^{i-1}}-1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, the hierarchical transformations are defined as decimating the even-numbered spins.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any vector <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the cyclic sum</p>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\text{cyc}}y_{j}y_{j+1}:=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell}y_{j}y_{j+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\text{cyc}}y_{j}y_{j+1}:=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell}y_{j}y_{j+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\ell+1}:=y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\ell+1}:=y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result demonstrates hierarchical invariance in this setting:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Parameter flow)<span id="Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the loss function takes the form of nearest-neighbor interactions, as commonly assumed in Ising models:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L(x)=-J\sum_{\text{cyc}}x_{j}x_{j+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>J</mi><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">cyc</mtext></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(x)=-J\sum_{\text{cyc}}x_{j}x_{j+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is called the interaction strength and we define <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{N+1}:=X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{N+1}:=X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the periodic boundary condition. Then, all distributions <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> have probability distributions of the similar nearest-neighbor form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\left(x^{(i)}\right)\propto\exp\left(\theta_{i}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(i)}_{j}x^{(i)}_{j+1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">cyc</mtext></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}\left(x^{(i)}\right)\propto\exp\left(\theta_{i}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(i)}_{j}x^{(i)}_{j+1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,\dots,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,\dots,d</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameters <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> evolve according to the recursive relations:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}=\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i-1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i}}\log\cosh\left(2\theta_{i-1}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}=\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i-1}}{2\bar{\sigma}_{i}}\log\cosh\left(2\theta_{i-1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the initial condition is given by</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}=\frac{\lambda J}{\sigma_{1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}=\frac{\lambda J}{\sigma_{1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}(x)\propto\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L({x})\right)=\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x_{j}x_{j+1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}}(x)\propto\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L({x})\right)=\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x_{j}x_{j+1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">We can derive</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\sum_{x_{2},x_{4},\dots,x_{N}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}x_{j+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\sum_{x_{2},x_{4},\dots,x_{N}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}x_{j+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{x_{2},x_{4},\dots,x_{N}}\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{x_{2},x_{4},\dots,x_{N}}\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\left(\sum_{x_{2j}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\left(\sum_{x_{2j}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">We use the fact that for all possible values of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1},s_{2}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1},s_{2}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>)</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\theta(s_{1}+s_{2}))+\exp(-\theta(s_{1}+s_{2}))=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\frac{1}{2}\log\cosh(2\theta)s_{1}s_{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cosh</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\theta(s_{1}+s_{2}))+\exp(-\theta(s_{1}+s_{2}))=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\frac{1}{2}\log\cosh(2\theta)s_{1}s_{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Applying this result, for any <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\dots,N/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\dots,N/2</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{x_{2j}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{x_{2j}}\exp\left(\theta_{1}\left(x_{2j-1}x_{2j}+x_{2j}x_{2j+1}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cosh</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}=\frac{1}{2}\log\cosh(2\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}=\frac{1}{2}\log\cosh(2\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{(1)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cosh</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)^{\frac{N}{2}}\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cosh</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)^{\frac{N}{2}}\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}x_{2j-1}x_{2j+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)^{\frac{N}{2}}\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cosh</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(2\cosh^{1/2}(2\theta)\right)^{\frac{N}{2}}\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\theta^{\prime}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">Thus,</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P^{(2)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P^{(2)}_{X^{(2)}}\left(x^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{1}\theta^{\prime}}{\bar{\sigma}_{2}}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{1}\theta^{\prime}}{\bar{\sigma}_{2}}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\exp\left(\theta_{2}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtext>cyc</mtext></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\exp\left(\theta_{2}\sum_{\text{cyc}}x^{(2)}_{j}x^{(2)}_{j+1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text">Using this argument inductively completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This hierarchical invariance provides a significant computational advantage even in sampling from the classical maximum entropy distribution, where <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\sigma}_{d}=\dots=\bar{\sigma}_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\sigma}_{d}=\dots=\bar{\sigma}_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a non-trivial observation. To efficiently sample from the distribution <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over the vast space <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, standard methods like Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) can be computationally expensive, often requiring the rejection of numerous initial samples to ensure proper mixing. Instead, by leveraging the self-similarity of the distribution, we propose an alternative approach that enables direct sampling without discarding any samples, significantly improving efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For fixed values of <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}=(x_{1},x_{3},\dots,x_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}=(x_{1},x_{3},\dots,x_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional distribution
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form</p>
<table id="S5.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N/2}X_{2j}(x_{2j-1}+x_{2j+1})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\left(\theta_{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N/2}X_{2j}(x_{2j-1}+x_{2j+1})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which factorizes as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}=\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp(\theta_{1}X_{2j}(x_{2j-1}+x_{2j+1}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(1)}_{X^{(1)}|X^{(2)}=x^{(2)}}=\prod_{j=1}^{N/2}\exp(\theta_{1}X_{2j}(x_{2j-1}+x_{2j+1})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Since this expression factorizes over the variables <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(1)}/X^{(2)}=\{X_{2},X_{4},\dots,X_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(1)}/X^{(2)}=\{X_{2},X_{4},\dots,X_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each spin <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{4}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{4},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be sampled independently as Bernoulli random variables. This allows for efficient sampling using the inverse transformation method, avoiding the need to discard samples. By using Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 14 (Parameter flow). ‣ 5.3 Nearest-Neighbors Loss Function ‣ 5 Hierarchical Invariances and Parameter Flows ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and applying this argument inductively, we conclude that sampling from <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> can be performed efficiently without rejection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, given <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an arbitrary distribution <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we study the Pareto optimal solutions of the related <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchical minimum relative entropy</em> problem:</p>
<table id="S6.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\argmin_{P_{X}:~\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}\bigl(D\bigl(P_{X^{(1)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(1)}}\bigr),D\bigl(P_{X^{(2)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(2)}}\bigr),\dots,D\bigl(P_{X^{(d)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(d)}}\bigr)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.548em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{P_{X}:~\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu}\bigl(D\bigl(P_{X^{(1)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(1)}}\bigr),D\bigl(P_{X^{(2)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(2)}}\bigr),\dots,D\bigl(P_{X^{(d)}}\big\|Q_{X^{(d)}}\bigr)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the pushforward measures when <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through the sequence of transformations <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}:=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}:=(T_{i})_{i=1}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Based on an argument similar to Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Hierarchical Maximum Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we analyze the associated hierarchical relative entropy regularization problem</p>
<table id="S6.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\argmin_{P_{X}}\left\{D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{P_{X}}\left\{D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and show how the solution can be obtained by a generalized version of the renormalization group. This formulation represents the Lagrangian for minimizing <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the mean constraint <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following generalization of the renormalization operator:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalized renormalization)<span id="Thmdefinition9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\theta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\theta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(1)}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(1)}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(2)}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(2)}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be continuous distributions on a set <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability density functions <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the generalized renormalization operator <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{X},\tilde{Z})=\mathcal{G}\bigl(P^{(1)}_{X},P_{X}^{(2)};\theta\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{X},\tilde{Z})=\mathcal{G}\bigl(P^{(1)}_{X},P_{X}^{(2)};\theta\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}:=\int_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1}(x))^{\theta}(p_{2}(x))^{1-\theta}\mathrm{d}x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}:=\int_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1}(x))^{\theta}(p_{2}(x))^{1-\theta}\mathrm{d}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution with the probability density function</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{X}(x):=\frac{(p_{1}(x))^{\theta}(p_{2}(x))^{1-\theta}}{\tilde{Z}},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{X}(x):=\frac{(p_{1}(x))^{\theta}(p_{2}(x))^{1-\theta}}{\tilde{Z}},\textrm{ for all }x\in\mathcal{A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The generalized renormalization operator is defined analogously for discrete random variables except by replacing the integrals with sums.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The distribution <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above is known as the <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized escort distribution</em> in the statistical physics literature; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of the following lemma, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Theorem 30]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. For any distributions <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{Q},\tilde{Z})=\mathcal{G}(Q_{1},Q_{2};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{Q},\tilde{Z})=\mathcal{G}(Q_{1},Q_{2};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> be the output of the generalized renormalization operator. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\theta D(P\|Q_{1})+(1-\theta)D(P\|Q_{2})=D(P\|\tilde{Q})-\log\tilde{Z}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta D(P\|Q_{1})+(1-\theta)D(P\|Q_{2})=D(P\|\tilde{Q})-\log\tilde{Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is the counterpart to the Gibbs variational principle for relative entropy:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a continuous set and function <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{Z}:=\int_{x\in{\mathcal{A}}}\exp\left(-\lambda f(x)\right)q_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x&lt;\infty." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{Z}:=\int_{x\in{\mathcal{A}}}\exp\left(-\lambda f(x)\right)q_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any distribution <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability density function <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]=D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right)-\log\tilde{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]=D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right)-\log\tilde{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution with probability density function</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp\left(-\lambda f(x)\right)q_{X}(x)}{\tilde{Z}},\textrm{ for all }x\in{\mathcal{A}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp\left(-\lambda f(x)\right)q_{X}(x)}{\tilde{Z}},\textrm{ for all }x\in{\mathcal{A}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can write</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[f(X)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x+\lambda\int_{\mathcal{A}}f(x)p_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x+\lambda\int_{\mathcal{A}}f(x)p_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{\exp\left(-\lambda{f(x)}\right)q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathcal{A}}p_{X}(x)\log\left(\frac{p_{X}(x)}{\exp\left(-\lambda{f(x)}\right)q_{X}(x)}\right)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right)-\log\tilde{Z}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=D\left(P_{X}\middle\|\tilde{P}_{X}\right)-\log\tilde{Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, it was shown that when <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of decimation transformations, the optimization problem (<a href="#S6.E2" title="Equation 6.2 ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) has a unique minimizer, which can be efficiently computed using the Marginalize-Tilt (MT) algorithm. Here, we extend this result to a much broader class of hierarchical transformations, proving that the solution remains unique and can be obtained using Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, a generalization of the MT algorithm, and we derive a condition on the temperature for which the mean constraint <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Again, we assume that all alphabets are standard Borel spaces, ensuring the existence of regular conditional probabilities and well-defined reverse random transformations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Generalized Renormalization Group</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">let</span> <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}:=\int\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}q_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}:=\int\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}q_{X}(x)\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          and <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(1)}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}q_{X}(x)}{Z_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(1)}_{X}(x):=\frac{\exp{\left(-\frac{\lambda}{\sigma_{1}}L(x)\right)}q_{X}(x)}{Z_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Initialization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1\text{ to }d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1\text{ to }d-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}\leftarrow(T_{i})_{\#}P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}}\leftarrow(T_{i})_{\#}P^{(i)}_{X^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Pushforward (coarse-graining)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(P^{(i+1)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Z_{i+1}\right)\leftarrow\mathcal{G}\left(U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Q_{X^{(i+1)}};\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(P^{(i+1)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Z_{i+1}\right)\leftarrow\mathcal{G}\left(U^{(i)}_{X^{(i+1)}},Q_{X^{(i+1)}};\frac{\bar{\sigma}_{i}}{\bar{\sigma}_{i+1}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Generalized renormalization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 17</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the definition of <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> identical to equations (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 4.2 ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
For any <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\Bigl(P_{X}\Big\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\Bigr)-\log Z(\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]=D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}\Bigl(P_{X}\Big\|\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}\Bigr)-\log Z(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof follows a similar structure to that of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, but instead of using Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Gibbs variational principle). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 4 Renormalization Group ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we apply Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. Similarly, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> can be viewed as a hierarchical extension of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 18</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The distribution <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}^{[\lambda]}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique solution to the minimization problem (<a href="#S6.E2" title="Equation 6.2 ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{P_{X}}\left\{D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}=-\log Z(\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{P_{X}}\left\{D_{(\sigma,\mathbf{T})}(P_{X}\|Q_{X})+\lambda\mathbb{E}[L(X)]\right\}=-\log Z(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We can similarly solve for <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> from the constraint given in the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 19</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Lagrange multiplier for which <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}_{X}^{[\lambda^{\ast}]}</annotation></semantics></math> solves the optimization problem (<a href="#S6.E1" title="Equation 6.1 ‣ 6 Hierarchical Minimum Relative Entropy ‣ Hierarchical Maximum Entropy via the Renormalization Group" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>). Then <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the condition</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=\mu." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\log Z(\lambda)\Big|_{\lambda=\lambda^{\ast}}=\mu.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduced the problem of hierarchical maximum entropy, extending the classical maximum entropy principle to systems with hierarchical structures. By addressing the interplay between uncertainty and constraints across multiple levels, this framework broadens the scope of maximum entropy methods to better capture structures that arise naturally in many real-world systems. We established a theoretical foundation for hierarchical maximum entropy and demonstrated that its solutions can be obtained through the renormalization group and disintegration operations. We also provided examples with hierarchical invariances, which significantly simplified the iterative renormalization group procedures by introducing the concept of parameter flows for quadratic modular loss functions, logarithmic loss functions, and nearest-neighbor loss functions. These examples illustrate some applications of the hierarchical maximum entropy framework. The insights from this work connect key ideas from probability theory, information theory, and statistical mechanics, offering a perspective for studying systems with hierarchical structures. Beyond theoretical contributions, this framework has the potential for applications in fields such as machine learning, statistical inference, and the analysis of physical and biological systems. Future research could explore additional classes of loss functions and alphabets, identify further instances of hierarchical invariances and computational efficiencies, investigate computational techniques for high-dimensional implementations, and extend the framework to incorporate alternative entropy measures. These advancements could enhance both the theoretical and practical impact of the hierarchical maximum entropy framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
